  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nThis afternoon, central Illinois is sandwiched between a \nsurface high centered over the mid-Atlantic and a surface low \nmoving across Ontario. Though the latter system is displaced \nfairly far to the north, the pressure gradient has tightened a \nlittle with a number of gusts to near 20 mph observed. This, \ncombined with relative humidity dropping into the 25-35% range, \nwill contribute to an elevated risk for agricultural field \nfires. This risk will be particularly notable south of about \nSpringfield-Mattoon, where conditions are classified as \nabnormally dry due to extended precipitation deficits. Moisture \npooling along a weak boundary moving in from the northwest on \nWednesday morning should keep RH values higher on Wednesday \nafternoon. Dry afternoon conditions should return each day from \nThursday through Saturday, continuing the elevated field fire\nrisk.\n\nOur next notable chance of rain hinges on the evolution of the\ndeveloping potential tropical system in the Gulf, and the track\nit eventually takes. Though variability is still high with the \ngreatest chances of heavy rainfall to our southeast from the \nGulf coast into the mid-South and the upper Ohio Valley, the EFI\nproduct shows a SoT contour encompassing southeastern Illinois,\nindicating that the top 10% of ensemble members are above the \n99th climatological percentile for QPF/forecast precipitation. \nLooking at the entire ensemble envelope, the chances of greater \nthan 0.50\" of precipitation at any given location in the\nsoutheastern portion of the ILX CWA with this event are only in\nthe 20-30% range. However, those high-end members are there, \nand this system bears watching.",
